  "//numbers": "The number drills, and the one section here that is not a list of cards — 1 to 1,000,000 cannot be enumerated, so app.js *composes* a reading out of these parts and the drills generate their prompts from it. `ones` are the digits 1–9 read alone; `alt` and `altk` are the other readings that are also right (四 is yon or shi, 七 nana or shichi, 九 kyuu or ku) — see below for which of the two is graded. `places` are 千 百 十 within a group of four digits, biggest first, each carrying the `forms` its own sound changes produce — 三百 is sanbyaku, 六百 roppyaku, 八千 hassen. `groups` are the multipliers above 9999. The difference between the two is the leading 一: a place drops it (10 is juu, never ichijuu) and a group keeps it (10,000 is ichiman, never man), which is the whole reason they are separate lists rather than one. Reaching 億 or 兆 is one more `groups` entry plus a wider band in NUM_BANDS; nothing else changes. A drill is `{id, kind, len, max, label, sample, subtitle}` — `kind` is \"count\" (1 to `max`, each value once) or \"random\" (`len` values dealt across the magnitude bands); `len` is how many prompts a run is. Which way round a prompt goes is the answer mode's to decide, never the drill's. `alt` is the romaji and `altk` the kana; Writing accepts either, so both are graded — see CLAUDE.md for why romaji is an answer here and never is for a deck. Note that neither is carried into a compound — 40 is yonjū and 400 yonhyaku, never shijū or shihyaku — so the drill teaches the counting forms rather than accepting everything that has ever been said.",
  "numbers": {
    "ones": [
      { "n": 1, "j": "一", "r": "ichi",  "k": "いち" },
      { "n": 2, "j": "二", "r": "ni",    "k": "に" },
      { "n": 3, "j": "三", "r": "san",   "k": "さん" },
      { "n": 4, "j": "四", "r": "yon",   "k": "よん",   "alt": ["shi", "yo"], "altk": ["し", "よ"] },
